@* One card in the process flow strip. The strip shows the PROCESSES in a folder;
   each one holds its own steps. See docs/design/02-process-dashboard.png.

   A plain button, NOT wrapped in a MudTooltip. Wrapping it in one swallowed the
   click - the tooltip root handles pointerup, and selecting a process stopped
   working entirely. The same failure as a button nested inside a MudMenu
   activator. The preview it used to host is rendered by the page instead, in a
   band under the strip: the rail scrolls horizontally, so anything positioned
   over a card gets clipped by its overflow anyway. *@

@* HOVER PREVIEW IS OFF.

   The two handlers that drove it are removed rather than commented out, and
   deliberately so: a Razor comment inside an element's attribute list is not a
   comment at all. Razor emits it as an attribute NAME, the browser throws
   InvalidCharacterError from setAttribute, and that kills the circuit on every
   render. This exact mistake took the page down earlier.

   To bring it back, put these two back on the button below, and nothing else
   needs changing - ProcessPage still listens and still renders the panel:

       @@onmouseenter="@@(args => OnPreviewWanted.InvokeAsync(
           new PreviewRequest(Process.Id, args.ClientX, args.ClientY)))"
       @@onfocus="@@(() => OnPreviewWanted.InvokeAsync(
           new PreviewRequest(Process.Id, null, null)))"

   If it does come back: mouseENTER, never mouseover. mouseover bubbles and
   re-fires every time the pointer crosses a child - the number, the name, the
   tag - so sweeping across the strip sent a storm of round trips down the
   circuit and the page stopped responding. And the focus handler is what makes
   the preview reachable by keyboard at all. *@
<button type="button"
        class="@CardClass"
        style="@TagStyle"
        aria-current="@(Selected ? "step" : null)"
        aria-label="@AriaLabel"
        @onclick="@(() => OnSelect.InvokeAsync(Process.Id))"
        @ondblclick="@(() => OnOpenDetail.InvokeAsync(Process.Id))">

    <div class="app-stepcard__top">
        <span class="app-stepcard__number">@Number</span>

        @* justification: the dot repeats the status, which is already spelled out
           in this card's aria-label. *@
        <span class="app-stepcard__dot"
              style="@($"background:{Process.StatusColour};")"
              aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>

    <div class="app-stepcard__name">@Process.Name</div>

</button>
